  "query_preview": "..Infrared satellite imagery this morning showed \nclear to partly cloudy skies across the area. Water vapor \nimagery showed tail end of upper trof moving through the \n4-corners with ridging pushing in from the southwest. Moisture \nremains in place across the area and with surface dewpoints in \nthe upper 50s to lower 60s this afternoon, widely scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms will develop mainly north and east of \nTucson.\n\nTo our west is our next significant weather maker with satellite\nimagery showing the upper low about 450 miles west of San \nFrancisco as of 230 am. This system will continue to move to \nS-SE over the next 48 hours and then move inland Wednesday night\nand become negatively tilted on Thursday while lifting to the \nE-NE. Southerly flow out ahead of this system will keep higher \nthan normal moisture values across the region. On Wednesday \nthere is an increased risk of severe thunderstorms, especially \nnorth of Tucson where SPC has this area painted in slight risk. \nHeavy rain is the other risk, especially northeast of Tucson \nwhere WPC Excessive Rainfall Outlook has this area painted as \nslight risk for flash flooding.\n\nThursday looks like it will be the more impactful day as the\nupper trof becomes negatively tilted. Heavy rain and flash\nflooding threat continues, especially Pinal, Graham and Greenlee\ncounties while the severe threat is minimized a bit due to cloud\ncover. WPC Excessive Rainfall outlook has areas NE of Tucson \npainted in slight risk, while on the severe side, most of the \narea has a marginal risk with this possibly being upgraded over \nthe next 48 hours.\n\nThe heaviest rainfall is expected to occur across Pinal, Graham\nand Greenlee counties where 48 hour NBM probabilities, ending\nThursday night, off greater than 1.50\" being 25-85% chance and \ngreater than 2\" being 20-60% chance. With that said and in\ncoordination with WFOs Phoenix and Flagstaff, a Flood Watch was\nissued Pinal, Graham and Greenlee counties has various time\nframes but mainly from late Wednesday morning until late\nThursday night. Overall QPF for Wed/Thu range from less 0.10\" \nfar west areas to over 1\" across Greenlee, Graham and eastern \nPinal counties with higher totals in the mountains due to \nupsloping.\n\nDrier air moves in for Friday, though some lingering showers \npossible near the NM border, which could linger into the weekend as \nwell. \n\nTemperatures through Thursday will be at or slightly above normal,\nthen cooler than normal Friday into the upcoming weekend. Latest\nNBM has possible first freeze of the season at the highest \nelevations of the White Mountains.",
